INV-zumin. `lookup_with_module` returns a single IoPrimitive and chains are
built from the one `io_boundary` string, so a primitive declared under several
boundaries kept exactly one -- decided by YAML ROW ORDER.
`scala.sys.process.Process.apply` is declared [fs_write, subprocess], tagged
fs_write, and the process launch was UNDETECTABLE AS A SUBPROCESS.

That loses four things the fs_write tag does not replace: a
{boundary: subprocess, must_not_exist: true} claim finds no chain; the
*** HIGH RISK *** marking never fires; the auto-derived taint sink lands in
zone host_fs instead of subprocess, so a "subprocess allowed, host_fs
prohibited" claim INVERTS; and INV-gahuz's opacity gate keys on the boundary.
The rows' own notes show the author knew both were true -- the fs_write note
reads "can write to filesystem via shell commands", which is a statement about
what the LAUNCHED PROGRAM does.

THE POPULATION IS THREE DIFFERENT THINGS AND ONLY ONE IS A DEFECT. Re-measured
across all fourteen catalogues with production's own loader; the filed counts
reproduce exactly (23 multi-boundary primitives, 27 unreachable declarations):

  (a) DISAMBIGUATED AT MATCH TIME  builtins.open picks by io_mode
                                   -> untouched, works as designed
  (b) UNDECIDABLE AT THE CALL SITE C unistd.write is fs_write OR net_send OR
                                   ipc_send by fd type -- EXACTLY ONE is true
                                   -> untouched DELIBERATELY
  (c) SIMULTANEOUSLY TRUE          scala Process.apply launches AND writes
                                   -> FIXED

(b) is the one worth being explicit about: multiplying those rows would
manufacture a net_send chain for every C write to stdout. That is a FALSE
VIOLATION -- the expensive direction -- and the honest fix there is fd-type
inference, which is its own item.

WHY A DATA MARKER AND NOT A ONE-LINE FALLBACK CHANGE. (a), (b) and (c) are
INDISTINGUISHABLE IN THE YAML -- all three read as "several rows, no mode" --
so nothing can infer which is which. Changing select_by_mode's
`return candidates[0]` could only swap WHICH declaration is silently lost. The
catalogue therefore has to SAY it: `simultaneous: true`, consumed at one
chokepoint.

ADDITIVE, so no existing consumer moves. `io_boundary` keeps its single-string
value and everything reading it -- the F3 gate, the taint sink derivation,
declares_opaque_crossing, third-party readers of the JSON -- is untouched. A new
`io_boundaries` list appears ONLY for a flagged primitive (4 of ~2,000 across
fourteen catalogues), and compute_boundary_map emits one chain per entry,
falling back to exactly one chain otherwise.

ONE HOME FOR THE CATALOGUE LOOKUP. The tagger needs to ask the catalogue a
SECOND question about the same match, and re-deriving which catalogue a dst
belongs to is NOT `dst.split(":")[0]` -- it runs through _resolve_ffi_catalog,
so a cgo call into `go:C:...` is answered by the C catalogue. A second copy
would get FFI edges wrong and drift the first time the redirect changed. So
classify_call is now a thin wrapper over classify_call_in_catalog, which
returns the match AND the catalogue it came from; the two cannot disagree about
what was matched.

TWO LANGUAGES, because a fix verified on one is not verified for another.
scala (fs_write+subprocess) and objc NSURLConnection (net_send+net_recv) fail on
different boundary pairs with different consequences. The objc rows ALREADY
ASSERTED simultaneity in prose -- "request implies both send and receive" --
with nothing consuming it, the same documented-and-unimplemented shape
builtins.open had before WI-rusof; inbound response data was never tracked as a
network read.

A VERDICT CHANGES, stated rather than discovered: an objc
{boundary: net_recv, must_not_exist: true} claim over code using those methods
goes confirmed -> violated. That is correct; an HTTP request does receive.

A HALF-DECLARED PAIR IS REJECTED, LOUDLY. The flag is a property of a PRIMITIVE
whose rows live in different YAML sections by construction (one per boundary),
so declaring it on one side only would make the marker live or inert depending
on which section a later editor updated -- the row-order dependence this
removes, wearing a new hat. simultaneous_boundaries_for raises on it, and a
parity test asserts no shipped catalogue is in that state.

A PARITY TEST CAUGHT ME ON ITS FIRST RUN, which is the second time this session
a gate has done so. My objc edit flagged a net_send row that grouped the two
genuinely-dual request methods WITH `connectionWithRequest:delegate:` and
`initWithRequest:delegate:`, which the net_recv row does not carry. The test
that rejects `simultaneous` on a single-boundary primitive fired; the row was
SPLIT rather than the guard weakened.

That split then made a test's NAME false --
test_a_flagged_row_with_one_boundary_is_not_simultaneous now reached its empty
result one branch earlier, so it no longer exercised the guard it was named
for. Renamed to what it actually asserts, and the guard got its own synthetic
test built deliberately NOT from load_catalog, since shipped data no longer
produces that shape and asserting it through a real catalogue would make the
test pass for the wrong reason the moment a catalogue changed.

Post-fix census: 4 primitives now reach every declaration; 17 remain
single-tagged and are classes (a), (b), the c/cpp stdio.fopen io_mode gap
(INV-kaduh, filed separately), haskell STM rows that are miscatalogued outright,
and 4 haskell System.IO rows left UNCLASSIFIED rather than guessed.

